Fillets: the cut of a rounding's end by a periodic face at the end is
split where it crosses the face's seam, also where the seam starts at the
corner's vertex (mitcad#330, with 0211).

PerformOneCorner looks for the crossing of the cut (the blend's curve on
the face at the end) with the face's seam by the extrema of the two 3d
curves, and takes it only where they meet within Precision::Confusion().
Where the seam starts at the corner's vertex, the cut passes it near its
highest point, and the two curves meet only within the cut's approximation
(2.4e-6 on a cylinder of radius 10 cut by a gable whose ridge ends on the
seam); on a stored body the cut may also pass a few hundredths of a
micrometre beyond the seam's start, within the vertex's tolerance. The
crossing was missed: the cut was stored unsplit, its curve on the face
running from one side of the seam to the other, the seam was lost, and
the face came out unorientable (or the body without its floor).

- ChFi3d_CutCrossesSeamLine: where the cut's curve on the face crosses the
  seam's line (an iso-line of the periodic parameter, up to a period).
- PerformOneCorner: where the extrema miss it, the cut is split at that
  crossing. Within the tolerance of one of the seam's end vertices it is
  split at that vertex, which keeps the whole seam (the pieces end at the
  vertex, no point is put on the seam); else at the point of the seam's
  curve there, as before. The two pieces' curves on the face are the cut's
  own, each in the period of the arc it starts or ends on.

